#{[#edb/ref 17592186050869 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125483} :activity/id #uuid "3a675a3e-a62b-5b11-9c31-522dea85c72d" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125815 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that the gradient of a function $f(x,y)$ is \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2xy-3x^2) \\ \\mathbf i + (x^2-2y) \\ \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\n \n\nHow can we reconstruct the function $f(x,y)$ knowing only its gradient?\n\nSince the components of $\\nabla f$ must be the first-order partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y$ respectively, we know that \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} =  2xy-3x^2,   \\qquad \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x^2-2y.}\n$$\n\nIntegrating  $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ as a constant, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\int \\left(2xy-3x^2\\right) \\textrm d  x = x^2y -x^3 +g(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(y)$ is an arbitrary function of $y.$ Intuitively, $g(y)$ contains all the terms that would vanish when differentiated with respect to $x.$\n\nNow, differentiating the above with respect to $y$ gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} = x^2 + g'(y).\n$$\n\nSo, equating the two expressions for $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{ \nx^2 + g'(y) &= x^2-2y\\ \n        g'(y)  & = -2y.\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle g(y)=\\int(-2y) \\ \\textrm d y =  -y^2 +C.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^2y -x^3-y^2 +C .\n$$\n\nNote that the above procedure is symmetric with respect to $x$ and $y.$ We could have started by integrating $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y$ and then differentiating with respect to $x.$ The final result would have been the same." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124569 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124378 :question/id #uuid "ff1853b3-627e-5c73-be98-b1d9a586f198" :question/math-academy-id "e-7020" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf{F},$ where $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2xy\\ \\mathbf i + (x^2 - 3)\\ \\mathbf j .$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}\\,\\textbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}}\\,\\textbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2-3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 + g'(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 -3$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & ={x}^{2}-3 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =-3 \\\\ g(y) & =-3y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=-3y +C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2y -3y + C.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Two-Variable Function From Its Gradient"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124574 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124382 :question/id #uuid "09d12c46-4ac8-53d9-80a6-ca68f98e0c5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7184" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)  = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = yz\\,\\mathbf{i} + (xz + 4z^2)\\,\\mathbf{j} + (xy+8yz)\\,\\mathbf{k}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} \\, \\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)  = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = yz, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int yz\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =xyz+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + \\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xz+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =xz+4{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =4{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z) = 4yz^2 + h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x, y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy + 8yz + h'(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy+8yz & =xy+8yz+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + C.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}}] :activity/title "Calculating Potential Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124740}] [#edb/ref 17592186051000 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134851} :activity/id #uuid "cc83e826-b428-5261-8109-f36f4450dc4f" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134851 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135122 :tutorial/content "Until now, we've been dealing with line integrals with respect to arc length. In this lesson, we will learn about other types of line integral. \n\nSuppose that $C$ is a path in the $xy$-plane with initial point $A$ and terminal point $B.$ The line integral of the function $f(x,y)$ **with respect to $\\boldsymbol x$** along the path $C$ is denoted\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo understand what this integral represents, we imagine traversing $C$ from $A$ to $B.$ At each point $P$ that lies on $C,$ we project the point on $f$ that lies directly above $P$ onto the plane $y=0.$ Doing this for every point on $C$ traces out a curve. The line integral of $f$ with respect to $x$ gives the (signed) area bounded by this curve and the $x$-axis.\n\nThis integral represents the total area accumulated when [math]f[/math] is projected on the plane [math]y=0[/math] as [math]C[/math] is traversed from the initial point [math]A[/math] to the terminal point [math]B.[/math] \n\n![](images/62/62c07a36cbf79459021ee7be125536a8f9c1bc3c193a99eabfcd3fc851b8eb0c.png)\n\nTo calculate a line integral with respect to $x,$ we must first parameterize $C$ using a parameter $t\\in [a,b].$ Then, we apply the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}x  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's take a look at a concrete example." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134852}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134852 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133891 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133707 :question/id #uuid "bbd835f9-a428-558c-9db7-838d02267a0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9512" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b5c788ad2c220b70a7f1d333680a70933f9b490a0db11fb3440dd3a2e06fa01.png)\n\nFind a definite integral that's equivalent to the line integral $\\displaystyle \\int_C (x+y) \\, \\textrm{d} x,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(3,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}x  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(3,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0+t(3-0),\\,0+t(3-0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t,\\,3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x+y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3t+3t \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6t\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to X"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134855}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134853 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133892 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133708 :question/id #uuid "7417f4d8-262c-5d16-9b35-f1c9710345e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9513" :question/problem "![](images/74/74033bfe17cefe62d5306b2186b9816ba26b816ece10c5dab5178abe1a9aaf89.png)\n\nFind the definite integral equivalent to the line integral $\\displaystyle\\int_C x^2 \\, \\textrm d y,$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $25x^2 + 4y^2 = 100,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,5),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 5\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}=4{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =20{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to Y"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134854}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133709 :question/id #uuid "e45bef0c-86ba-5030-bb0c-274df22673f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9521" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_C \\left(x^2 + y^2\\right)\\,\\textrm d y,$ where $C$ is the path along the upper-right quarter of the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 4,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8fc3a36829c9780c34e13f253d607c3849bfd3ef1399a77a79a3dd3d30ef7140.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135123 :tutorial/content "The **line integral with respect to** $\\boldsymbol y$ of the function $f(x,y)$ along the path $C$ is denoted\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nThis integral represents the total area accumulated when $f$ is projected on the plane $x=0$ as $C$ is traversed from the initial point $A$ to the terminal point $B.$ \n\n![](images/9c/9ccbabd17ff18511ed26e8098b2fad41104bbd921a32eb639ae1a74bcab6f94b.png)\n\nTo calculate a line integral with respect to $y,$ we must first parameterize $C$ using a parameter $t\\in [a,b].$ Then, we apply the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Line Integrals With Respect to Y"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134853}}] :activity/title "Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133995}] [#edb/ref 17592186050983 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167691} :activity/id #uuid "adc0eea0-4c11-5878-9c7e-48c4aba23d56" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168082 :tutorial/content "A vector field $\\mathbf F$ is **conservative** if there exists a scalar function $f$ such that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\nabla \\! f.\n$$\n\nIf such a function $f$ exists, it is called a **potential function** for $\\mathbf F.$\n\nFor example, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2x\\,\\mathbf i + 2y\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$ is a potential function for $\\mathbf F$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\  & =F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNot all vector fields are conservative. However, conservative vector fields have many interesting and useful properties, as we'll soon discover." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167698}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167698 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166846 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166696 :question/id #uuid "6ddfa412-1f5a-5ddd-8d61-d7f27b106022" :question/math-academy-id "e-8751" :question/problem "For which vector field is $f(x,y) = \\sin(2x-y)$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf F = \\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = 2\\cos(2x-y)\\,\\mathbf i -\\cos(2x-y) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vector Field Corresponding to a Given Potential Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166863 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166716 :question/id #uuid "3f266f66-1d79-5864-b497-1bc1b138c318" :question/math-academy-id "e-9499" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2?$\n\n- $\\mathbf F = (e^{y^2} - 2xy) \\, \\mathbf i + (2xye^{y^2} - y) \\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf G = (3x^2 - y^2) \\, \\mathbf i + (4 - 2xy) \\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf H = (xy^2 + 1) \\, \\mathbf i + 2xy \\,\\mathbf j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ we have $P = e^{y^2} - 2xy$ and $Q = 2xye^{y^2} - y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2ye^{y^2} - 2x, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2ye^{y^2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf G,$ we have $P = 3x^2 - y^2$ and $Q = 4 - 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = -2y, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = -2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{G}$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf H,$ we have $P = xy^2 + 1$ and $Q = 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf{H}$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166864 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166717 :question/id #uuid "ae01904d-13f0-5d6e-ad3a-cc714c65765b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9500" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\arctan{3x} \\, \\mathbf j$ on $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ the domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ and we have\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2}, \\qquad Q = \\arctan{3x}.\n$$\n\n \nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{2a}{1 + 9x^2}, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{3}{1 + 9x^2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on $\\mathbb R^2.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a}{1+9{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{1+9{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2a & =3 \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a = \\dfrac{3}{2}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parameter in a Given Conservative Vector Field"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168124 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that this is equivalent to saying that the scalar curl is zero everywhere on $D,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0.$$\n\nIf $D\\neq\\mathbb R^2,$ then the situation gets a little trickier. We'll consider this case in a separate lesson." :tutorial/title "A Test to Determine Whether a Given Vector Field is Conservative"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167748}}] :activity/title "Conservative Vector Fields in the Cartesian Plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166975}] [#edb/ref 17592186049147 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180497} :activity/id #uuid "ff0789e0-f094-5fc5-ac25-230d7c590b14" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179739 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179601 :question/id #uuid "d5a9cb43-6396-5153-a60c-3d653d61a47b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9536" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d51d0cea4b75dffe5eef6ccf46e5156716eb1381d20b4193d811acfe83278094.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the curve and constant vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =                 0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =  0$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                                 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $\\mathbf F$ works with the particle for $x >        0,$ while $\\mathbf F$  works against the particle for $x <           0.$ \n\n![](images/67/673798bb131da05a07b3f47894340cbbc143fa6a248823bcfbdf6b5a5238efb4.png)\n\n \nBy symmetry, the contribution to $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ for $x <                 0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x > 0.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =     0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/24/24884d31b2b9aa8d94b11bccfaab1fa31e62a34f4e5183676c79390923f1f243.png)\n\n \nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =      0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Constant Vector Field"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180503}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180497 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180720 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ and $C$ is a smooth curve in the $xy$-plane. Recall that the line integral\n\n$$\nW = \\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$\n\nrepresents the total work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C.$ Loosely speaking:\n\n- if $W > 0,$ then $\\mathbf F$ causes the particle to *speed up* as it moves along $C,$ and\n\n- if $W < 0,$ then $\\mathbf F$ causes the particle to *slow down* as it moves along $C.$\n\nTo understand this, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the path $C$ defined by the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ shown below. \n\n![](images/2a/2a3b3896767e5f6beb4eec39f44afcc2387e178df0cec0ebbbdaa5e4cb68e964.png)\n\n \n\nIt's helpful to imagine that $\\mathbf F$ represents a uniform gravitational field near the earth's surface. \n\nLet's consider two examples.\n\n- If a particle moves from $A$ to $B$ along the segment, it will pick up speed because it moves *with* the vector field:\n\n![](images/0f/0f0bbc24f7ed26390643546bf578b88a879da58f56ac0b817159deeea25f164b.png)\n\n \nIntuitively, this makes sense. We know from real-world experience that if we drop a pebble, its speed increases as it descends. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW = \\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.\n$$\n\n- If a particle moves from $B$ to $A$ along the segment, it will slow down because it moves *against* the vector field:\n\n![](images/2e/2eabe985ee8b360ef79841561547b2251a818e63e53e91785a83d0c2cf3609e3.png)\n\nAgain, this matches our intuition: If we toss a pebble into the air, its speed decreases as it ascends.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW = \\int\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r < 0.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180498 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179742 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179604 :question/id #uuid "d844a573-49a1-59c3-bec4-e409c486469d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9542" :question/problem "![](images/29/298b7b4293ff104a62107670fa2aba603b8a23e9ad042f7e270a4acf6267a2fa.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <  0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =         0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r >  0$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                     0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/cd/cd71f379a02405bc21aecfb0bb14e9798cfbc4d7fd2c1d3a1d5221aa8d64638f.png)\n\n \nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/b2/b2284e7ffa962fd46333c4eca47a77473007fdd96e0813b9928f0303df8170f3.png)\n\n \nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Non-Constant Vector Field"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180723 :tutorial/content "In some situations, a particle's speed increases and then decreases (or vice-versa) as it moves along a curve in a vector field. \n\nLet's consider a particle moving from $A$ to $B$ along the arc of a semicircle centered at $O$ in a uniform gravitational field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/46/466bd1716e626f6e3cc7859589974c27d49bdaa77bed9aa5813ef4bd9f3328e9.png)\n\n \n\nNote that:\n\n- as the particle moves from $A$ to $T,$ it works against $\\mathbf F$ and slows down, and\n\n- as the particle moves from $T$ to $B,$ it works with $\\mathbf F$ and speeds up.\n\nBy symmetry, the contribution to $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ for $x <                   0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x > 0.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =     0.\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this means that the particle's speed is the same at the points $A$ and $B,$ and these are the points of maximum speed. \n\nAdditionally, since $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ is decreasing as we move from $A$ to $T,$ the particle moves at its slowest speed at the point $T.$" :tutorial/title "Cases When a Particle Experiences Acceleration and Deceleration"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180493}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180724 :tutorial/content "Another noteworthy situation is when ![](images/c7/c75d4b37611dd632920692d430870b4d582fed06ac19178ccf69eb7c7dcb264a.svg) is perpendicular to ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) at all points along ![](images/27/278f99df1e14fe0ebfb61bdb72262a62670dcaa60d322148bc3ee591b278ec2d.svg)\n\n![](images/f3/f3f8b96e06c9e802e038130db69356924df9c9a385dc913e55cb5d9cc3a697b1.png)\n\n \n\nSince the force ![](images/c7/c75d4b37611dd632920692d430870b4d582fed06ac19178ccf69eb7c7dcb264a.svg) acts perpendicular to the particle's motion as it moves from ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) it neither helps nor hinders its motion. Therefore,\n\n![](images/06/06b0f05fc417c4572bb7763607ef1d471efc1282067b22c372f23caba33fcb19.svg)\n\nIn particular, if ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) is a level curve of a scalar field ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg) and ![](images/c5/c5b03af80a954012283edf6f027c02c7fe387d08ee6424e6b92186d580a65e7e.svg) then\n![](images/b3/b3dddb4aa6f178a9ab5ec8245e4881b260d60cfba33123b56b8b5ea99cda95d8.svg)" :tutorial/title "Cases When the Vector Field and Curve are Perpendicular"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180498}}] :activity/title "Interpreting Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179848}]}
